      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "First, we need to determine the total distance that Ryosuke's car drove from when he picked up his friend at work until he dropped his friend off at home. To do this, we subtract the initial odometer reading from the final odometer reading:\n\n74,592 miles - 74,568 miles = 24 miles\n\nNext, we need to calculate how many gallons of gas were used to drive that distance. We know that Ryosuke's car gets 28 miles per gallon, so we divide the distance driven by the car's fuel efficiency:\n\n24 miles / 28 miles per gallon = 0.857 gallons\n\nNow that we know the car traveled 0.857 gallons of gas, we need to determine the cost of that gas. We know that the price of one gallon of gas is $4.05, so we multiply the number of gallons by the price per gallon:\n\n0.857 gallons * $4.05 per gallon = $3.47\n\nTherefore, the cost of the gas that Ryosuke used for his car to drive his friend back home from work is $3.47, rounded to the nearest cent.\n\nThe answer is: 3.47",
